[' Here is a simple C code that takes input from command line arguments, converts the second argument (index 1) to an integer using `atoi()` function and then prints the argument at the specified index using the given function `print()`. Finally, it returns 0 and exits the program.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

void print(int index) {
    char** argv = (char**) malloc((sizeof(char*)));
    int argc = 1; // assuming the first argument is the program name
    char* arg = getenv("argv");
    if (arg == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: No command line arguments\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    const char delimiter[] = {" ", 0};
    char* token = strtok(arg, delimiter); // tokenize the arguments
    while (token != NULL) {
        argv[argc] = token;
        argc++;
        token = strtok(NULL, delimiter);
    }

    if (index >= 1 && index < argc) {
        printf("%s\n", argv[index]);
    } else {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Invalid argument index\n");
    }

    free(argv);
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 3) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s index\n", argv[0]);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    int index = atoi(argv[1]);
    print(index); // call the print function with the specified index

    return 0;
}
```

This program expects you to pass it the index as a command line argument. For example: `./your_program 2` will output the second command line argument (argument at position 2).

You can compile this C code with `gcc`:

```sh
$ gcc your_program.c -o your_program
```

And run it as follows:

```sh
./your_program 2 "Hello, World!"
```

This will output the string "World" to the console since index 2 is the second word of "Hello, World!".']

[' To create a simple C function similar to `atoi()` but using command line arguments, you can follow this example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

int my_atoi(char* str) {
    int sign = 1, number = 0;

    while(*str == ' ') str++;

    if (*str == '-') {
        sign = -1;
        str++;
    }

    while (isdigit(*str)) {
        number = number * 10 + (*(str++) - '0');
    }

    return sign * number;
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if(argc < 2) {
        printf("Usage: ./a.out argument\n");
        return 1;
    }

    int result = my_atoi(argv[1]);
    printf("The integer value of the argument is: %d\n", result);

    return 0;
}
```

This code defines a function `my_atoi()` that converts a given string to an integer and a main function which takes in command line arguments, calls `my_atoi()` on the first argument (argv[1]), and prints the result.

To compile this program, save it as `a.c`, navigate to the directory containing the file, and run:

```sh
gcc -o a a.c
```

Then you can execute the compiled binary with command line arguments:

```sh
./a 42
The integer value of the argument is: 42
```

or

```sh
./a -123
The integer value of the argument is: -123
```']

