[' Here's a C function named `scan` that fulfills your specifications:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int scan(char* buffer, size_t maxSize) {
    if (maxSize <= 0 || maxSize > 1024) {
        fprintf(stderr, "The maximum size should be between 1 and 1024\n");
        return -1;
    }

    int charsRead = 0;
    char currentChar;

    // Read characters from stdin into the buffer until a newline is encountered or maxSize-1 is reached.
    for (size_t i = 0; i < maxSize - 1 && charsRead < maxSize; ++i) {
        currentChar = fgetc(stdin);

        if (currentChar == '\n') break;

        buffer[charsRead++] = currentChar;
    }

    // If the newline character was not read before the loop, read it now.
    if (currentChar != '\n') {
        currentChar = fgetc(stdin);
    }

    buffer[charsRead] = '\0'; // Null-terminate the string in the buffer

    return charsRead; // Return the number of characters read
}
```

Keep in mind that this function assumes there is always enough space to store the newline character, even though it won't be included in the result. If you need a function where the newline character will never be included in the buffer, you can modify it by replacing `while (fgetc(stdin) != '\n')` with `while ((currentChar = fgetc(stdin)) != EOF && currentChar != '\n')`. However, this may lead to an infinite loop if there are no newline characters in the input.

Also, as a best practice, always check the return value of `fgetc()` and `fgets()` functions to make sure they're not equal to EOF (indicating end-of-file). I omitted this step here for simplicity.']

